      "text": "To find the greatest possible three-digit number that is divisible by both 3 and 6, we need to determine the greatest three-digit number and then check if it meets the criteria.\n\n1. Identify the greatest three-digit number:\n   The greatest three-digit number is 999.\n\n2. Verify divisibility by 3:\n   A number is divisible by 3 if the sum of its digits is divisible by 3. Let's check the sum of the digits of 999:\n   \\[\n   9 + 9 + 9 = 27\n   \\]\n   Since 27 is divisible by 3, 999 is divisible by 3.\n\n3. Verify divisibility by 6:\n   A number is divisible by 6 if it is divisible by both 2 and 3. We already know that 999 is divisible by 3. However, we need to check if it is divisible by 2. \n   The number 999 is an odd number, so it is not divisible by 2. Therefore, 999 is not divisible by 6.\n\nSince 999 is not divisible by 6, we need to find the next greatest three-digit number that is divisible by both 3 and 6. We do this by finding the greatest three-digit number divisible by 6. The greater multiples of 6 are:\n   \\[\n   996, 993, 990, \\ldots\n   \\]\n   To find the greatest three-digit multiple of 6, we divide 999 by 6 and round down to the greatest integer less than or equal to the quotient:\n   \\[\n   999 \\div 6 = 166.5\n   \\]\n   The integer part is 166, so the greatest three-digit multiple of 6 is:\n   \\[\n   166 \\times 6 = 996\n   \\]\n   We check the digits of 996 to confirm it is divisible by 3:\n   \\[\n   9 + 9 + 6 = 24\n   \\]\n   Since 24 is divisible by 3, 996 is divisible by 3. Therefore, 996 is also divisible by both 3 and 6.\n\nThus, the greatest possible three-digit number that is divisible by both 3 and 6 is \\(\\boxed{996}\\).",
