      "text": "To find the greatest possible three-digit number that is divisible by \\(3\\) and \\(6\\), we start by noting that a number divisible by \\(6\\) must be divisible by both \\(2\\) and \\(3\\). Therefore, we need to find the greatest three-digit number that is divisible by \\(6\\).\n\nFirst, identify the greatest three-digit number, which is \\(999\\). We need to check if \\(999\\) is divisible by \\(6\\). A number is divisible by \\(6\\) if it is divisible by both \\(2\\) and \\(3\\).\n\n1. **Divisibility by \\(2\\):**\n   A number is divisible by \\(2\\) if its last digit is even. The last digit of \\(999\\) is \\(9\\), which is odd. Therefore, \\(999\\) is not divisible by \\(2\\). This means \\(999\\) cannot be divisible by \\(6\\).\n\nSince \\(999\\) is not divisible by \\(6\\), we need to find the largest number less than \\(999\\) that is divisible by \\(6\\).\n\n2. **Finding the largest number less than \\(999\\) that is divisible by \\(6\\):**\n   To find the largest multiple of \\(6\\) less than \\(999\\), we divide \\(999\\) by \\(6\\) and take the greatest integer less than or equal to the quotient.\n   \\[\n   999 \\div 6 = 166.5\n   \\]\n   The greatest integer less than or equal to \\(166.5\\) is \\(166\\). Therefore, the largest multiple of \\(6\\) less than \\(999\\) is:\n   \\[\n   6 \\times 166 = 996\n   \\]\n   We can verify that \\(996\\) is divisible by \\(6\\) by checking its divisibility by \\(2\\) and \\(3\\):\n   - It is even, so it is divisible by \\(2\\).\n   - The sum of the digits of \\(996\\) is \\(9 + 9 + 6 = 24\\), which is divisible by \\(3\\), so \\(996\\) is divisible by \\(3\\).\n\nThus, the greatest possible three-digit number that is divisible by \\(3\\) and \\(6\\) is \\(\\boxed{996}\\).",
